  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The supplied formula gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>54</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow></math>. Three sides in an arithmetic progression therefore have form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, where d is a positive integer. Triangle feasibility gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Take the side 12 as a horizontal base. Area 54 gives perpendicular height 9. Let the signed horizontal coordinate of the opposite vertex from the left base endpoint be t, with adjacent side lengths <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> on the left and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> on the right.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Pythagoras gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>81</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>81</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Subtracting gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>144</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>24</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>48</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> Substitute back: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>81</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, yielding sides 9,12,15. The shortest is 9, answer C.</p><p><strong>5.</strong> The resulting right triangle has area 54 and perimeter 36, and the given relation returns inradius 3. This independently checks all original conditions. Signed t makes the height equations valid even before knowing whether its foot lies inside the base.</p>"
